"[CSSWG] Minutes Lisbon F2F 2016-09-19 Part V: Transforms, Variables, Transitions/Animations, Cascade, CR Plan, January Meeting [css-transforms] [css-variables] [css-transitions] [css-animations] [css-cascade] from Dael Jackson on 2016-11-16 (www-style@w3.org from November 2016) (https://lists.w3.org/Archives/Public/www-style/2016Nov/0073.html)\nciteturn2369view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2363view4\",\"lineno\":320}); Total lines: 725\nL222:     Value-tainting and allowing var() in keyframes\nL231:       TabAtkins: However, (Shane can give more detail), we've\nL232:                  experimented with just tainting values directly\nL233:       TabAtkins: And apparently this is extremely easy.\nL234:       TabAtkins: Would like to, if possible, do value-based tainting\nL235:                  instead of weird name-based tainting.\nL236: \nL237:       birtles: Does this also cover the display property?\nL238:       birtles: Could you use a custom property to also animate display?\nL239:       TabAtkins: Only to go from none to block.\nL240:       TabAtkins: Shouldn't bring up new issues.\nL241:       TabAtkins: Only 'display: none' has special animation behavior.\nL242: \nL243:       <frremy> --foo: abc;\nL244:                animation: var(--foo);\nL245:                @keyframes abc { to { --foo: def } }\nL246:       dbaron: I agree that fremy's case is an issue\nL247:       TabAtkins: This ties into the next issue, which is about using\nL248:                  var() in @keyframes\nL249:       TabAtkins: Several engines allow var() in @keyframes even though I\nL250:                  didn't spec it that way.\nL251:       TabAtkins: Need to define doing var() substitutions twice for this.\nL252:       TabAtkins: In this example, need to substitute var(foo) in the\nL253:                  animation, get the abc value.\nL254:       TabAtkins: Then run the keyframe, would transition to def value.\nL255:       TabAtkins: At which point you redo your variable substitutions,\nL256:                  but not in the keyframes.\nL257:       dbaron: That's painful for dynamic changes.\nL258:       dbaron: If there's a dynamic change, need to go back to the\nL259:               original one...\nL260:       dbaron: Or keep 2 sets of selectors for each element\nL261:       shane: You need to do that anyway.\nL262:       TabAtkins: Not generally, but if you are doing var in keyframes,\nL263:                  which apparently you do.\nL264:       TabAtkins: Then you do.\nL265: \nL266:       dbaron: I'm not sure we did it intentionally.\nL267:       TabAtkins: var() in keyframes takes the value from the element the\nL268:                  animation is running on.\nL269:       TabAtkins: To do that properly, need to do that once, then\nL270:                  resolve, then resubstitute.\nL271:       TabAtkins: Or just do it once and animate a property that doesn't\nL272:                  get reflected, very weird.\nL273:       dbaron: I feel like you're crossing var() in keyframes and\nL274:               --custom-prop in keyframes.\nL275:       dbaron: Problem you described is problem with having custom-prop\nL276:               in keyframes, not having var()\nL277:       TabAtkins: It's a problem with having custom properties in\nL278:                  keyframes that affect animations, currently handled by\nL279:                  tainting.\nL280:       TabAtkins: In ideal non-problematic case, have custom property\nL281:                  defined for element, animation of that property on\nL282:                  keyframes rule\nL283:       TabAtkins: var substitution should reflect the running animation,\nL284:                  not the base value.\nL285:       dbaron: Animations make you compute style.\nL286:       fantasai: How do you animate a string?\nL287:       TabAtkins: flip at 50%\nL288:       * fantasai doesn't think this is useful\nL289:       fantasai: what's the use case for animating custom properties?\nL290:       TabAtkins: Adding ability to type custom properties in Houdini.\nL291:       TabAtkins: Lea also has some examples where it's useful to animate\nL292:                  keywords.\nL293: \nL294:       <birtles> problem with display property:\nL295:                 --foo: block;\nL296:                 animation: yer 1s;\nL297:                 display: var(--foo)\nL298:                 @keyframes yer { from, to { --foo: none } }\nL299: \nL300:       dbaron: How does the global tainting work?\nL301:       TabAtkins: If a custom property is animated in any keyframe\nL302:       TabAtkins: That name is tainted, and becomes an invalid reference\nL303:                  in an animation-* property.\nL304:       dbaron: I think global tainting is less disruptive cuz affects\nL305:               less stuff.\nL306:       shane: Can be bad because blow a way a bunch of rules unexpectedly.\nL307:       dbaron: Can you do the other tainting in both directions?\nL308:       dbaron: Such that if you use a custom property in a keyframe then\nL309:               you taint the value and it can't be used in an animation\nL310:               and... that's harder though.\nL311:       dbaron: To solve frremy's case, need to also taint in other\nL312:               direction,\nL313:       shane: For frremy's case\nL314:       shane: You apply your static style\nL315:       shane: That might trigger some animations\nL316:       shane: And then you apply your animated style after that.\nL317:       shane: Don't update var() references in animation properties.\nL318:       dbaron: As long as we don't send AnimationStart and AnimationEnd\nL319:               events each time.\nL320:       shane: You don't update animation-* properties EVER as a result of\nL321:              animated styles.\nL322:       dbaron: That's actually really hard for us.\nL323:       dbaron: When we apply the animation style, we create a new style\nL324:               context with a different rule, and the old one is gone now.\nL325:       dbaron: We're relying on the way cascading works that it has the\nL326:               same animation values\nL327:       dbaron: And this would mean sometimes they don't.\nL328:       TabAtkins: Don't update them.\nL329:       dbaron: How do we not update them?\nL330:       shane: You...\nL331:       dbaron: We recompute the variable references because we created a\nL332:               new style context.\nL333:       Rossen: Our impl is fairly similar in that respect.\nL334:       frremy: You cannot animate custom properties that are used in\nL335:               animation.\nL336:       frremy: If you use something in animation, big red flag to not use\nL337:               it.\nL338:       TabAtkins: One component might be using animation name for\nL339:                  animation, another using it for something completely\nL340:                  different. They could be in different subtrees, b/c\nL341:                  this is global.\nL342:       shane: Could maybe look into subtree-scoping... but need to\nL343:              inherit that down...\nL344:       dbaron: I guess we could do it by keeping the pre-animation style\nL345:               around and get the non-animation properties from that.\nL346:       dbaron: Not that crazy.\nL347:       dbaron: We'd then end up... I just don't want to use that trick\nL348:               much\nL349:       dbaron: Each time we use that trick, it's another power of 2 in\nL350:               memory... already using it for :visited stuff.\nL351:       fantasai: power of 2 for whole tree?\nL352:       dbaron: No just that element, but also adds lots of complexity.\nL353: \nL354:       TabAtkins: Relatedly, I would love to note any idea you have wrt\nL355:                  handling var in keyframes today.\nL356:       TabAtkins: For that still need these multiple phases.\nL357:       TabAtkins: Do need to run var substitution once to know what\nL358:                  you're animating\nL359:       TabAtkins: And then need to ...\nL360:       dbaron: We're just computing style twice\nL361:       TabAtkins: That works, that's multi-phase -- but nobody needs to\nL362:                  remember what the old style was in that case.\nL363:       dbaron: We're only doing it twice when you initially do the\nL364:               animation\nL365:       dbaron: Or when keyframes applied are changed.\nL366:       dbaron: Otherwise we have right animation the first time through.\nL367:       dbaron: But also have the keyframes the first time through.\nL368: \nL369:       TabAtkins: Ok, I would like to at least just keep an issue on the\nL370:                  name-tainting that we should try to figure out\nL371:                  something better.\nL372:       TabAtkins: Works well for our impl, not so much for y'alls. Would\nL373:                  like something cleaner and safer.\nL374:       dbaron: I guess your proposal isn't that horrible, just need to\nL375:               have the other style around.\nL376:       dbaron: Might even be able to get away without it.\nL377: \nL378:       TabAtkins: Alternative is to not allow var() in animations ever.\nL379:       TabAtkins: Don't know what uses show up right now. But could\nL380:                  restrict it entirely.\nL381:       birtles: Common to use variable for animation durations, to set it\nL382:                globally.\nL383:       TabAtkins: Makes sense.\nL384:       <leaverou> TabAtkins, I recently used var() in animation\nL385:                  properties. The animation was simulating typing by\nL386:                  animating width with steps(), so I used var(--length)\nL387:                  in both steps(), and in animation-duration (multiplied\nL388:                  by .2s)\nL389: \nL390:       TabAtkins: So, dbaron, how much \"not that horrible\" do you feel\nL391:                  this is.\nL392:       TabAtkins: Should we switch the spec to value-tainting, or as an\nL393:                  issue.\nL394:       dbaron: Would prefer to keep as an issue until someone implements.\nL395:       gregwhitworth: Raised issue because we want to know what to do.\nL396:       TabAtkins: var in keyframes is a separate issue.\nL397:       TabAtkins: This was just about animation-tainting.\nL398:       TabAtkins: Let's talk about that issue.\nL399:       TabAtkins: Proposed resolution is the var() function works in\nL400:                  @keyframes rules, and pulls its values from the element\nL401:                  being animated.\nL402:       TabAtkins: Specifically, from the before-animations-run value.\nL403:       Rossen: Any objections?\nL404:       leaverou: If you use --foo in a keyframe, and var() in a keyframe,\nL405:                 does it take its value from the element or the keyframe?\nL406:       TabAtkins: From the base value. You set up the animation keyframes\nL407:                  first, and then ????\nL408:       TabAtkins: Everything else uses the animated value.\nL409:       [Everyone is confused]\nL410:       leaverou: var(--foo) will reflect the base value in the animation\nL411:                 but not elsewhere? what?\nL412:       TabAtkins: It'll work like Lea wants, where var() will be replaced\nL413:                  based on custom property in the keyframe.\nL414:       fantasai: Which is not what you said in the beginning.\nL415:       TabAtkins: It is not.\nL416:       <gregwhitworth> and I'm assuming that the --foo inside of the\nL417:                       keyframe will update the values outside of the\nL418:                       @keyframe correct?\nL419: \nL420:       TabAtkins: Objections to proposed resolution?\nL421:       TabAtkins: Proposed resolution: var() function works in keyframes,\nL422:                  pulls its value from the animated value of the custom\nL423:                  property on the element\nL424:       RESOLVED: var() function works in keyframes, pulls its value from\nL425:                 the animated value of the custom property on the element.\nL426: \nL427:       SimonSapin: So any cycles are resolved the usual way?\nL428:       TabAtkins: Yeah\nL429:       TabAtkins: Only remaining issue is, don't change\nL430:                  animation-tainting yet, but may want to change to\nL431:                  value-tainting but too expensive atm.\nL432:       dbaron: Might want to describe what value-tainting is in that note.\nL433:       fantasai: Add an issue or a note\nL434: \nL435:       birtles: The display issue.\nL436:       TabAtkins: That's a separate issue. Please file it.\nL437:       TabAtkins: Always been an issue.\nL438:       dbaron: ... been an issue since we added ability for keyframes to\nL439:               set non-animatable properties\nL440: \nL441:     Animations\nL442:     ==========\nL443:       Scribe: TabAtkins\nL444: \nL445:       birtles: We have level 2 specs for animations and transitions,\nL446:                delta specs.\nL447:       birtles: They define some extra events - animationcancel,\nL448:                transitioncancel, transitionstart (Edge and IE10 ship).\nL449:       birtles: I went to ship in Firefox, but spec and IE differ in\nL450:                delay. After some discussion, I added transitionrun as\nL451:                well.\nL452:       birtles: Lets us tell if a transition actually fired or not.\nL453:       birtles: transitionstart in Edge fires at the end of the delay,\nL454:                matching animationstart. Want to make the spec match.\nL455:       birtles: Then add transitionrun, which fires when a transition is\nL456:                applied, even if it's in a delay period.\nL457:       birtles: So we were thinking that since Edge ships we can just\nL458:                implement that, and do transitionrun at the same time.\nL459:                And then might as well do the cancels.\nL460:       birtles: So should we go for it?\nL461: \nL462:       birtles: Issue is the elapsedTime member, a little complicated.\nL463:       birtles: elapsedTime wasn't clear, behavior or use-case. Was it\nL464:                for syncing animations?\nL465:       birtles: The way it's specced doesn't let you do that. It gives\nL466:                you the ideal time of when the event was fired, not the\nL467:                actual time.\nL468:       birtles: But all browsers are consistent, and spec says that. I've\nL469:                left it as it is.\nL470:       birtles: So should cancel events use the same thing - time when\nL471:                animation was canceled? Or zero?\nL472:       SimonSapin: If you're making it 0, does it need to be there at all?\nL473:       birtles: It's on the interface they all share.\nL474:       birtles: It's used enough in GitHub that we can't just remove it.\nL475:       <birtles> 【5†https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/68†github.com】\nL476:       TabAtkins: Is it used in a way that would break things if we just\nL477:                  made it 0 all the time?\nL478:       dino: Useful in iteration events, to know what iteration you're in.\nL479:       TabAtkins: Maybe we can cheat and just return multiple of duration\nL480:                  in the iteration event, zero elsewhere?\nL481:       birtles: The start/end events do so too, so you can just divide by\nL482:                duration.\nL483:       birtles: A lot of uses are just dumping to console for no obvious\nL484:                benefit.\nL485: \nL486:       TabAtkins: So the cancels get fired if the transition property is\nL487:                  removed?\nL488:       birtles: Yeah.\nL489:       TabAtkins: Or if the target-value is changed? We cancel the\nL490:                  current, start a new transition?\nL491:       birtles: Yeah.\nL492:       TabAtkins: Okay, then we probably do want the elapsedTime, to know\nL493:                  whether we were nearly done with the transition or near\nL494:                  the beginning.\nL495:       TabAtkins: So we need a resolution - do we want to bless these\nL496:                  four events as shippable?\nL497:       SimonSapin: Should we move them to the level 1 spec?\nL498:       TabAtkins: What level is Transitions/Animations at? WD?\nL499:       [looks, they're both 2013 WDs]\n"
